{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Zebulun, at the shore of seas he shall dwell.</b> In his land, for he shall inherit a coastal region. He placed Zebulun, who engages in commerce, before Issachar, who engages in Torah — and so did Moses our teacher in his blessing, saying: שְׂמַח זְבוּלֻן בְּצֵאתֶךָ וְיִשָּׂשכָר בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ (Deuteronomy 33:18). For indeed it is impossible to engage in Torah unless a person first secures his livelihood, as they said: אִם אֵין קֶמַח אֵין תּוֹרָה (Avot 3:17). And when one supports his fellow to provide his livelihood so that he may engage in Torah, as they said of Zebulun, then the service of the blessed El through the exertion of the one engaging in Torah is attributed to both of them. This was also the intent of the Torah in the gifts of priesthood and Levite — that all the people support those who grasp the Torah, namely the priests and Levites, as it says: יוֹרוּ מִשְׁפָּטֶיךָ לְיַעֲקֹב (Deuteronomy 33:10) — so that all may merit eternal life, as they said: all Israel have a share in the world to come (Sanhedrin 90a)." "<b>And he is at the shore of ships.</b> Outside his land he goes to the shore of ships for kinds of merchandise — such as fish, purple-dye shellfish, and white glass — which were found in its seas and on its sand, as Scripture testified concerning him, saying: כִּי שֶׁפַע יַמִּים יִינָקוּ וּשְׂפוּנֵי טְמוּנֵי חוֹל (Deuteronomy 33:19)."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:35:24.876582420Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}